olicy out of executive discretion and into federal law. That would make the framework harder for future administrations to reverse quickly.\n","newText":"ARMA, short for the American Reserve Modernization Act, would direct the US Treasury to establish a formal Bitcoin reserve and a separate stockpile for other federally held digital assets. The bigger goal is to move reserve policy out of executive discretion and into federal law, making the framework harder to reverse quickly.\n"},{"oldText":"It matters because it changes how the market may view government-held Bitcoin. If reserve BTC cannot be sold quickly, traders have less reason to treat that stash as immediate potential supply. It also signals that lawmakers want Bitcoin treated as a long-duration strategic asset.\n","newText":"It matters because it changes how the market may view government-held Bitcoin. If reserve BTC cannot be sold quickly, traders have less reason to treat that stash as immediate potential supply. It also signals that lawmakers want Bitcoin treated as a long-duration strategic asset.\n"},{"oldText":"The research set cites roughly 328,372 BTC in federal hands, mostly accumulated through seizures and forfeitures. At current price levels, that is worth more than $25 billion. The dollar value changes with the market, but the size of the stash is already large enough to influence reserve policy debates.\n","newText":"The research set cites roughly 328,372 BTC in federal hands, mostly accumulated through seizures and forfeitures. At current price levels, that is worth more than $25 billion. The stash is already large enough to influence reserve policy debates.\n"},{"oldText":"No. Some coverage says ARMA echoes the earlier BITCOIN Act's vision of acquiring up to 1 million BTC over five years in a budget-neutral way, but that is still a legislative objective. Traders should wait for final statutory language and Treasury implementation details before treating that figure as committed sovereign demand.\n","newText":"No. Some coverage says ARMA echoes the earlier BITCOIN Act's vision of acquiring up to 1 million BTC over five years in a budget-neutral way, but that is still a legislative objective. Traders should wait for final statutory language before treating that figure as committed sovereign demand.\n"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":2644,"output":3047,"cacheRead":13952,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":19643,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779433604579,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiEFx6d7uvJvX3vJnBlxTbUFeSE3z"}}
{"type":"message","id":"b0a7221a","parentId":"134856e2","timestamp":"2026-05-22T07:07:16.291Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_JuZGtlGicPz2MTl8QJ3qt2zU","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 12 block(s) in /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-064848/article/draft_raw.md."}],"details":{"diff":"     ...\n  11 # Strategic Bitcoin Reserve bill targets a 20-year US BTC lock\n  12 \n  13 The fight over a **Strategic Bitcoin Reserve** is moving from executive messaging to proposed law. Reporting from [CoinTelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound) and [Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law) says the American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA, would direct the US Treasury to create a formal Bitcoin reserve and a separate stockpile for other federally held digital assets.\n  14 \n- 15 The key market detail is the holding rule. Bitcoin assigned to the reserve would need to stay there for at least 20 years, unless it is sold to reduce national debt. That is a sharper framework than the government's past pattern of auctioning seized coins as cases closed.\n+ 15 The key detail is the holding rule. Bitcoin assigned to the reserve would need to stay there for at least 20 years, unless it is sold to reduce national debt. That would mark a break from the government's habit of auctioning seized coins.\n  16 \n- 17 For traders, the bill matters because it reframes federal Bitcoin from disposable inventory into a strategic balance-sheet asset. If that policy survives the legislative process, it could reduce assumptions around future government selling while opening a fresh debate about sovereign accumulation.\n+ 17 For traders, the bill matters because it reframes federal Bitcoin from disposable inventory into a strategic asset. If it advances, assumptions around future government selling could change.\n  18 \n  19 ## Why the Strategic Bitcoin Reserve debate looks different now\n  20 \n  21 ### A statute has more weight than an executive order\n  22 \n- 23 The most important shift in ARMA is legal durability. An executive order can launch a policy, but a later administration can narrow it, delay it, or reverse it. Congress writing reserve treatment into law would make the framework harder to unwind and easier for markets to model over several cycles.\n+ 23 The most important shift in ARMA is legal durability. An executive order can launch a policy, but a later administration can narrow it or reverse it. Congress writing reserve treatment into law would make the framework harder to unwind and easier for markets to price.\n  24 \n- 25 That distinction matters because the United States already controls a large amount of Bitcoin through seizures and forfeitures. What it has lacked is a stable rulebook for whether those coins should be sold, warehoused, or treated as part of a national reserve strategy. ARMA tries to answer that question directly.\n+ 25 That matters because the United States already controls a large amount of Bitcoin through seizures and forfeitures. What it has lacked is a stable rulebook for whether those coins should be sold, warehoused, or treated as part of a national reserve strategy.\n  26 \n- 27 For market participants, statutory treatment changes time horizon. It tells investors to think less about the next auction headline and more about whether Washington wants Bitcoin sitting alongside other strategic stores of value on a longer schedule.\n+ 27 For market participants, statutory treatment extends the time horizon. It shifts focus from the next auction headline to whether Washington wants Bitcoin on the balance sheet for years.\n  28 \n  29 ### The 20-year lock changes the supply story\n  30 \n- 31 The 20-year minimum hold is the clearest line in the bill. It would limit the government's ability to turn reserve Bitcoin into near-term supply every time price or politics changes. That alone could alter how traders think about a meaningful stash of publicly known BTC.\n+ 31 The 20-year minimum hold is the clearest line in the bill. It would limit the government's ability to turn reserve Bitcoin into near-term supply whenever price or politics shifts.\n  32 \n- 33 It is still too early to price the proposal as guaranteed supply removal. The bill must move through Congress, survive amendment, and then be implemented in a way that actually transfers holdings into the reserve structure. But if the core hold rule stays intact, the market gets more certainty around federal custody behavior.\n+ 33 It is still too early to price this as guaranteed supply removal. The bill must pass Congress and be implemented in a way that actually transfers holdings into the reserve structure. But if the hold rule survives, the market gets more certainty around federal custody behavior.\n  34 \n- 35 There is also a signal effect. A government that commits to holding Bitcoin for two decades is treating it less like evidence from an enforcement action and more like strategic financial inventory.\n+ 35 It also sends a message. A government that commits to holding Bitcoin for two decades is treating it less like evidence and more like strategic financial inventory.\n  36 \n  37 ## What ARMA would change inside the Treasury\n  38 \n  39 ### One reserve for Bitcoin, one stockpile for everything else\n  40 \n- 41 ARMA appears designed to separate Bitcoin from the rest of the government's digital asset pile. Under the proposal, Treasury would maintain a strategic Bitcoin reserve while other federally held tokens would sit in a different digital asset stockpile. That split is politically useful and analytically cleaner for markets.\n+ 41 ARMA appears designed to separate Bitcoin from the rest of the government's digital asset pile. Under the proposal, Treasury would maintain a strategic Bitcoin reserve while other federally held tokens would sit in a different stockpile.\n  42 \n- 43 Bitcoin is being framed here as reserve-grade collateral, not just another token on a confiscation ledger. A separate structure for non-Bitcoin assets reduces confusion over what counts as sovereign Bitcoin exposure and what remains a mixed basket gathered through legal actions.\n+ 43 Bitcoin is being framed here as reserve-grade collateral, not just another token on a confiscation ledger. A separate structure for non-Bitcoin assets reduces confusion over what counts as sovereign Bitcoin exposure.\n  44 \n- 45 Research cited across the source set says the federal government already holds 328,372 BTC worth more than $25 billion, mostly from seizures and forfeitures. If that figure is close to the mark, the United States is already one of the largest nation-state Bitcoin holders. ARMA is less about creating exposure from zero and more about defining what an existing federal position should become.\n+ 45 Research cited across the source set says the federal government already holds 328,372 BTC worth more than $25 billion, mostly from seizures and forfeitures. If that figure is close to the mark, ARMA is less about creating exposure from zero and more about defining what an existing federal position should become.\n  46 \n  47 ### Audits and self-custody widen the bill's appeal\n  48 \n- 49 The legislation is not only about accumulation or long holds. It also tries to answer two recurring industry complaints: opaque custody and federal overreach. The proposal includes quarterly proof-of-reserve reports, independent third-party audits, and congressional oversight.\n+ 49 The legislation is not only about accumulation or long holds. It also tries to answer two recurring complaints: opaque custody and federal overreach. The proposal includes quarterly proof-of-reserve reports, independent third-party audits, and congressional oversight.\n  50 \n- 51 Those measures matter because reserve narratives fall apart when the market cannot verify balances or custody standards. A public reporting cadence would not remove political risk, but it would make surprise inventory moves harder and give traders a cleaner way to track federal holdings.\n+ 51 Those measures matter because reserve narratives weaken when the market cannot verify balances or custody standards. Public reporting would not remove political risk, but it would make surprise inventory moves harder and give traders a cleaner way to track federal holdings.\n  52 \n- 53 ARMA also reportedly protects lawful self-custody. That gives the bill a second lane of support beyond reserve politics, because it pairs state accumulation rules with a property-rights message that many digital asset investors care about.\n+ 53 ARMA also reportedly protects lawful self-custody. That pairs reserve policy with a property-rights message that many digital asset investors care about.\n  54 \n  55 ## What traders should watch next\n  56 \n  57 ### The headline is strong, but the mechanics are slower\n  58 \n- 59 The reserve concept is powerful enough to move sentiment, but traders should separate symbolism from execution. A bill moves through committees, negotiations, scorekeeping, and legal drafting. That process is slower than an executive announcement and much less certain.\n+ 59 The reserve concept is powerful enough to move sentiment, but traders should separate symbolism from execution. A bill moves through committees, negotiations, and legal drafting. That process is slower than an executive announcement and much less certain.\n  60 \n  61 The open question is how budget-neutral expansion would work. Coverage says the bill, like the earlier BITCOIN Act, contemplates acquiring up to 1 million BTC over five years without taxpayer money. Until lawmakers define the mechanism, that should be treated as policy ambition rather than an active purchase plan.\n  62 \n- 63 A few checkpoints matter most:\n+ 63 Key checkpoints:\n  64 \n  65 - Whether the 20-year hold language survives unchanged.\n  66 - Whether Treasury gets a clear process for transferring seized Bitcoin into reserve custody.\n  67 - Whether acquisition plans remain budget-neutral or become politically diluted.\n  68 - Whether reporting rules are detailed enough to make reserve balances easy to verify.\n  69 \n- 70 There is also room for internal conflict. Some officials have praised the idea of a national Bitcoin reserve, while others have sounded cautious on agency buying. If ARMA gains traction, price response will likely depend less on the headline alone and more on the final language around purchases, debt exceptions, and custody transfer.\n+ 70 There is also room for internal conflict. Some officials have praised the idea of a national Bitcoin reserve, while others have sounded cautious on agency buying. If ARMA gains traction, price response will depend on the final language around purchases, debt exceptions, and custody transfer.\n  71 \n  72 ## Conclusion\n  73 \n- 74 The **Strategic Bitcoin Reserve** debate is no longer just a campaign-era talking point. ARMA turns it into a concrete question about statutory custody, 20-year holding discipline, audits, and the role Bitcoin could play on the US balance sheet. Traders should track committee progress and the bill's purchase mechanics closely, because those details will decide whether this story stays symbolic or becomes a real shift in sovereign Bitcoin supply.\n+ 74 The **Strategic Bitcoin Reserve** debate is no longer just a talking point. ARMA turns it into a concrete question about statutory custody, 20-year holding discipline, audits, and the role Bitcoin could play on the US balance sheet. Traders should track committee progress and purchase mechanics closely, because those details will decide whether this story stays symbolic or becomes a real shift in sovereign Bitcoin supply.\n  75 \n  76 ## FAQs\n  77 \n  78 **1. What is the ARMA bill trying to do?**\n  79 \n- 80 ARMA, short for the American Reserve Modernization Act, would direct the US Treasury to establish a formal Bitcoin reserve and a separate stockpile for other federally held digital assets. The bigger goal is to move reserve policy out of executive discretion and into federal law. That would make the framework harder for future administrations to reverse quickly.\n+ 80 ARMA, short for the American Reserve Modernization Act, would direct the US Treasury to establish a formal Bitcoin reserve and a separate stockpile for other federally held digital assets. The bigger goal is to move reserve policy out of executive discretion and into federal law, making the framework harder to reverse quickly.\n  81 \n  82 **2. Why does the 20-year holding rule matter so much?**\n  83 \n  84 It matters because it changes how the market may view government-held Bitcoin. If reserve BTC cannot be sold quickly, traders have less reason to treat that stash as immediate potential supply. It also signals that lawmakers want Bitcoin treated as a long-duration strategic asset.\n  85 \n  86 **3. How much Bitcoin does the US already hold?**\n  87 \n- 88 The research set cites roughly 328,372 BTC in federal hands, mostly accumulated through seizures and forfeitures. At current price levels, that is worth more than $25 billion. The dollar value changes with the market, but the size of the stash is already large enough to influence reserve policy debates.\n+ 88 The research set cites roughly 328,372 BTC in federal hands, mostly accumulated through seizures and forfeitures. At current price levels, that is worth more than $25 billion. The stash is already large enough to influence reserve policy debates.\n  89 \n  90 **4. Does this mean the government will buy 1 million BTC soon?**\n  91 \n- 92 No. Some coverage says ARMA echoes the earlier BITCOIN Act's vision of acquiring up to 1 million BTC over five years in a budget-neutral way, but that is still a legislative objective. Traders should wait for final statutory language and Treasury implementation details before treating that figure as committed sovereign demand.\n+ 92 No. Some coverage says ARMA echoes the earlier BITCOIN Act's vision of acquiring up to 1 million BTC over five years in a budget-neutral way, but that is still a legislative objective. Traders should wait for final statutory language before treating that figure as committed sovereign demand.\n  93 \n  94 **Sources:**\n  95 - CoinTelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\n  96 - Decrypt: https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\n     ...","firstChangedLine":15},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779433636291}}
